content.text:
Interesting: `screenScroll: 0` because the mission screen is hidden (display:none). `gridH:0`, `innerH:0`. Body scroll = 900 exactly → no page scrollbar now. 

So the scrollbar in the checkmap screenshot was the mission screen's own scrollbar: screenScroll when open. The checkmap session was on port 3003 with the 1-lane test level and the mission screen open; scrollHeight might be >900 because the mission inner has min-height 852 but the screen padding... Let me measure with the screen open. In the checkmap screenshot, the scrollbar thumb was long (from 55 to 400 of ~600 visible). Hmm.

Wait, checkmap's screenshot is 1024x600? Maybe the read tool scaled it. The page viewport 1280x900.

Actually, `.mission-screen` is fixed inset 0, `overflow:auto`, and `.mission-inner` min-height 852; content 625; so scrollHeight should be 900 (not larger). Then no scrollbar. Unless `100vh` in a fixed element with padding... 900-48=852 + 48 padding = 900. Exactly, no scroll.

But the screenshot showed a scrollbar. Maybe that's the *browser window* scrollbar because the game page is 900 (exactly) but the mission screen is fixed and the body still has a scrollbar? Body scroll=900 = viewport → no scrollbar (Chromium hides when equal).

Hmm, but in the checkmap run, the page was at 1280x900 but the browser window originally launched at 1280x900 with `--window-size`. The metrics override may not have been applied in checkmap (I didn't set it), so the viewport was the window's content area, maybe 1280x750 due to the headless window chrome? No chrome in headless. The checkmap screenshot aspect looked 1280x750 scaled to 1024x600 → yes! 1280x750 * 0.8 = 1024x600. So the checkmap run had a 750px-tall viewport (no metrics override in that script). At 750 height, the mission screen content (625 + padding 48 = 673 < 750?) Hmm still fits. But the min-height 852 > 750 → inner is 852 → scrollHeight 900 > 750 → scrollbar! That explains it.

So at 900px there's no scrollbar. At shorter viewports, the mission screen scrolls — intended. Fine. No fix needed. But the min-height forcing 852 causes scrolling on short screens even if content fits (625). Better: remove the forced min-height and instead use `margin-top:auto` on the footer with `min-height:100%`? Simplest: change min-height to `calc(100vh - 48px)` only when it doesn't cause overflow... it inherently does on <700px viewports (which is most laptops at ~700-800 usable). Content 625 + 48 = 673, so for 750 it fits without min-height. The min-height makes it 900 → scrollbar. Better to drop the min-height and instead use `.mission-inner{min-height:calc(100vh - 48px)}` only on tall screens? Or simply remove it — the footer then sits right after the grid, leaving background below. The footer "PROGRESS SAVES AUTOMATICALLY" would float right below the content rather than at the page bottom. That's acceptable and avoids phantom scrolling.

Alternative: `.mission-inner{min-height:0}` on desktop but use `flex:1` with the footer margin-top:auto requires a height. Instead, put the footer right after the grid. I'll remove the min-height and adjust: `.mission-screen{display:flex;flex-direction:column}` with `.mission-inner{flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;min-height:0}`, footer margin-top:auto — but the screen is the scroll container with height 100%; inner flex:1 gives it the full height, footer auto → pinned, no overflow unless content exceeds. With padding-bottom, inner flex:1 fills available 852; content 625; footer pinned bottom. That's exactly the same as min-height but without forcing overflow? No — flex:1 also makes inner at least the available height, same result: 852 + 48 padding = 900. The scrollbar came from viewport 750 where min-height 852 > available 702. With flex, inner would be 702 and no overflow (content 625 fits). So flex is the right approach.

Implement:
```css
.mission-screen{...;display:flex;flex-direction:column}
.mission-inner{...;flex:1 0 auto;min-height:0;...}
```
Hmm, `flex:1 0 auto` = grow 1, shrink 0, basis auto → basis = content height (625), grow to fill if space; if content > available, it stays content height and the screen scrolls. That's ideal. Also `justify-content` not needed because footer has margin-top:auto.

But the base `.mission-inner{min-height:calc(100vh - 48px)}` must be replaced. Since `.mission-screen` is currently not display:flex. Let me override in a new appended block:

```css
.mission-screen{display:flex;flex-direction:column}
.mission-inner{flex:1 0 auto;min-height:0}
```
But `.mission-screen[hidden]{display:none}` remains (defined earlier with higher specificity than `.mission-screen`? `.mission-screen[hidden]` specificity (0,2,0) vs `.mission-screen` (0,1,0) → hidden wins even if later. Good.

Also the mobile media query sets `.mission-inner{min-height:calc(100vh - 30px)}` — override with min-height:0 in the same appended block, but the mobile media query comes before my new block? My new block is appended last, so `.mission-inner{min-height:0}` wins over the media rule (same specificity, later). Good, but for mobile I might want the footer pinned too; flex works.

Let me add that.

Then, mobile mission screen at 844 height: content: header 60 + hero ~200 + 6 cards ~150 each = 900 + footer → taller than viewport → scrolls. Fine.

Now, other refinements:

1. Mission screen desktop empty space: with flex the footer will be at the bottom but the grid ends ~490, leaving ~250px blank. Could add a "field note" panel under the grid to fill: e.g., a horizontal strip showing the plant roster with lock states ("YOUR SEED BANK") and the next unlock. That would be a meaningful addition showcasing progression! Let's add a "SEED BANK" strip:

